ZOO.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Gallery of Fine Arts, Split","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.515234,"lo":16.438068,"d":1258,"ds":"The Museum of Fine Arts (Croatian: Galerija umjetnina), is an art museum in Split, Croatia containing works from the 14th century up to the present day providing an overview of the artistic developments in the local art scene.","wpu":"Gallery of Fine Arts, Split","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Split_-_Gallery_of_Fine_Arts.JPG/960px-Split_-_Gallery_of_Fine_Arts.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Co-Cathedral of St. Peter, Split","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.5102,"lo":16.4564,"d":1491,"ds":"The Co-Cathedral of St. Peter (also called Split Co-Cathedral; Croatian: Konkatedrala sv. Petar Apostola) is a Catholic church located in Split, in the southern part of Croatia, which serves as the co-cathedral of the Archdiocese of Split-Makarska.","wpu":"Co-Cathedral of St. Peter, Split","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Split-Konkathedrale-1.jpg/960px-Split-Konkathedrale-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ivan Meštrović Gallery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.504306,"lo":16.417917,"d":1781,"ds":"Ivan Meštrović Gallery (Croatian: Galerija Meštrović), is an art museum in Split, Croatia dedicated to the work of the 20th-century sculptor, Ivan Meštrović. The gallery preserves and presents to the public the most significant works of Meštrović, and is in itself an art monument. The permanent collection includes works of sculpture, drawings, design, furniture and architecture.","wpu":"Ivan Meštrović Gallery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split-Mestrovic-Galerie-01.jpg/960px-Split-Mestrovic-Galerie-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Church of the Holy Trinity, Split","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.5209,"lo":16.4358,"d":1909,"ds":"Church of Holy Trinity (Croatian: Crkva sv. Trojstva) is a Pre-Romanesque style Roman Catholic church located in Split, Croatia. Out of all early-medieval (old Croatian) architectural monuments in Dalmatia, which historians date back to the period between 8th and 11th century, Church of Holy Trinity, with its original shape and rich findings, has a very important place.","wpu":"Church of the Holy Trinity, Split","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Split-Kirche-SvTrojice-3.jpg/960px-Split-Kirche-SvTrojice-3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dalmatia Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.520278,"lo":16.451111,"d":2019,"ds":"Dalmatia Tower (Croatian: Toranj Dalmacija) is a high-rise office building and a hotel in Split, Dalmatia, Croatia. It is located on the intersection of the Domovinskog rata Street and the Dubrovačka Street and it is the tallest building in Croatia, with the height of 135 m with antenna.","wpu":"Dalmatia Tower","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d8/Westgate_Tower_B_opening_light_show_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Brodosplit","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.523611,"lo":16.441667,"d":2184,"ds":"Brodosplit is the largest shipyard in Croatia, located in the Supaval bay, on the north side of the Split peninsula.","wpu":"Brodosplit","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/29/Brodosplit_Logo.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Lora (Split)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.526389,"lo":16.428056,"d":2669,"ds":"Lora is a harbor in the northwestern part of Split, Croatia, in the Bay of Kaštela. It is currently used as a naval base of the Croatian Navy. Located right next to the Brodosplit shipyard, it has been an important naval base for the Yugoslav Navy (1945–1991) and its headquarters.","wpu":"Lora (Split)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Split_OpenStreetMap.png/960px-Split_OpenStreetMap.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Žnjan","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.508143,"lo":16.48435,"d":3606,"ds":"Žnjan is a city kotar and the largest beach in Split, Croatia. It is located in the eastern part of the city. Like most of the Croatian coast, it is mostly a pebble beach.","wpu":"Žnjan","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/%C5%BDnjan_District_%28Split%29.svg/960px-%C5%BDnjan_District_%28Split%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Aqueduct of Diocletian","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.524167,"lo":16.489444,"d":4574,"ds":"The Aqueduct of Diocletian (Croatian: Dioklecijanov akvadukt) is an ancient Roman aqueduct near Split, Croatia (Latin: Spalatum) constructed during the Roman Empire to supply water to the palace of the emperor Diocletian, who was Augustus 284 to 305 AD, retired to Spalatum, and died there in 311.","wpu":"Aqueduct of Diocletian","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Split-Diokletian-Aqueduct-17.jpg/960px-Split-Diokletian-Aqueduct-17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hollow Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.5403,"lo":16.497,"d":6116,"ds":"Hollow Church (Croatian: Šuplja crkva) is the archaeological excavation of what used to be a Romanesque Roman Catholic church in the 11th-century Salona, Croatia.","wpu":"Hollow Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Crkva_sv._Petra_i_Mojsija_u_Solinu.jpg/960px-Crkva_sv._Petra_i_Mojsija_u_Solinu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cambi castle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.55,"lo":16.383,"d":6876,"ds":"Cambi Castle is castle in Kaštel Kambelovac, a town within the administrative area of Kaštela in Dalmatia, Croatia.","wpu":"Cambi castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Ka%C5%A1tel_Kambelovac%2C_Croatia_20231217_06.jpg/960px-Ka%C5%A1tel_Kambelovac%2C_Croatia_20231217_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Rižinice monastery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.5549,"lo":16.4988,"d":7382,"ds":"Rupotine is an archeological site near Solin, Croatia, which is believed by Croatian archaeologists to be the site of medieval Rižinice monastery. It is considered one of the most significant early medieval Croatian archeological sites due to the inscription of duke Trpimir \"PRO DUCE TREPIME(ro)\" which was found here.","wpu":"Rižinice monastery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Ri%C5%BEinice%2C_Solin%2C_Croatia_20231226_20.jpg/960px-Ri%C5%BEinice%2C_Solin%2C_Croatia_20231226_20.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Vitturi Castle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.555,"lo":16.368,"d":8114,"ds":"The Vitturi Castle is a 15th-century castle in Kaštel Lukšić, a town within the administrative area of Kaštela in Dalmatia, Croatia.","wpu":"Vitturi Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/HR-Kastell-Vitturi-2.jpg/960px-HR-Kastell-Vitturi-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Slatine","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.5,"lo":16.333333,"d":8614,"ds":"Slatine is a village on Čiovo, an island in Split-Dalmatia County, Croatia. It is a small fishing and tourist village situated on the northeastern side of the island Čiovo, only 8 km from Trogir. It lies on the coast of Kaštela bay, opposite Split and peninsula Marjan, which are to the east.","wpu":"Slatine","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Slatine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Kaštel Stari","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.55,"lo":16.35,"d":8877,"ds":"Kaštel Staris a settlement within the town of Kaštela in Dalmatia, Croatia. Its name means Old Castle, this came from a nobleman from Trogir, Koriolan Cipiko, writer, humanist and military leader who built a mansion in 1476 which it is believed to have led to current name.","wpu":"Kaštel Stari","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/HR-Kastel-Stari-1.jpg/960px-HR-Kastel-Stari-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fortress of Klis","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.56,"lo":16.523889,"d":9192,"ds":"The Klis Fortress (Croatian: Tvrđava Klis; Italian: Fortezza di Clissa) is a medieval fortress situated above the village of Klis, near Split, Croatia.","wpu":"Fortress of Klis","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Klis_iz_zraka.jpg/960px-Klis_iz_zraka.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Castle Rotondo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.547553,"lo":16.33545,"d":9721,"ds":"Castle Rotondo is a fortress castle in Kaštel Štafilić, Croatia, on the Adriatic coast. It was fully constructed in 1508, on the order of Stefano Štafileo, a Trogirian nobleman, to protect his lands and the Kozjak's peasants from the Ottomans pillaging. It is situated by a beautiful sea cliff.","wpu":"Castle Rotondo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ka%C5%A1tel_%C5%A0tafileo%2C_Ka%C5%A1tel_%C5%A0tafili%C4%87%2C_20220130_08.jpg/960px-Ka%C5%A1tel_%C5%A0tafileo%2C_Ka%C5%A1tel_%C5%A0tafili%C4%87%2C_20220130_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Split (; Croatian: [splît] ) is the second-largest city in Croatia, after the capital Zagreb, situated on the eastern shore of the Adriatic Sea. As the administrative seat of Split-Dalmatia County and the largest city on the Croatian coast, it serves as a key economic, cultural, and transportation hub for the region. Developed around the historic core of Diocletian's Palace and extending across a central peninsula toward its surrounding suburbs and hinterland, Split combines a continuous urban tradition spanning more than seventeen centuries with a contemporary role as one of Croatia's leading tourist destinations.","wpu":"Split, Croatia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Split_080620-133710-IMG_0968x.jpg/960px-Split_080620-133710-IMG_0968x.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}